## MOTIVATION

- **The Helmholtz operator is sign-indefinite and oscillatory.** The BVP for $(\Delta + k^2)u = -f$ is not coercive: the associated bilinear form loses positive-definiteness as the wavenumber $k$ grows, so standard elliptic solver theory — and the contraction arguments underpinning diffusion-type Monte Carlo (WoS/WoSt) — do not carry over.
- **The pollution effect makes high-frequency solves scale super-linearly.** For standard FEM/FDM, holding a fixed number of degrees of freedom per wavelength does *not* bound the error as $k\to\infty$. Babuška & Sauter proved this pollution is fundamental: *"we will prove that, in two and more space dimensions, it is impossible to eliminate this so-called pollution effect"* (SIAM Review 42, 2000, pp. 451–484; orig. SIAM J. Numer. Anal. 34(6):2392–2423, 1997) — so DOF counts must grow faster than $k^d$ to hold accuracy.
- **Global solves scale poorly with frequency and dimension.** Discretizing a $d$-dimensional domain at $\sim 10$ points per wavelength gives $O(k^d)$ unknowns; the resulting indefinite systems are ill-conditioned and hostile to both direct factorization and Krylov iteration absent specialized preconditioning.
- **Wavefield storage is prohibitive for large-scale inverse problems.** Adjoint/FWI-style optimization must store or recompute the full forward wavefield across all grid points and frequencies; memory scales as $O(k^d)$ per field and is the dominant bottleneck at scale.
- **Monte Carlo methods offer structural relief: grid-free, output-sensitive, embarrassingly parallel, no wavefield storage.** As Sawhney & Crane (2020) established for WoS, walk-based estimators give *"no meshing, trivial parallelism, and the ability to evaluate the solution at any point without solving a global system of equations"* — directly attacking the storage and pollution bottlenecks by evaluating $u$ only at query points.
- **But naive Neumann-series WoB fails for Helmholtz.** Writing the boundary integral equation as $(I-A)\mu = b$ and expanding $\mu = \sum_{n\ge 0} A^n b$ requires spectral radius $\rho(A) < 1$. At moderate/high frequency the indefinite Helmholtz kernel gives $\rho(A) \ge 1$, so the series diverges and deep walk composition suffers **unbounded variance**. This is exactly the failure mode that motivated the Convergent Born Series: Osnabrugge et al. (2016) note the classical Born series *"diverges for larger structures, or structures with a high scattering contrast"* (convergent only when all eigenvalues of $G_0 V$ have magnitude $<1$).

