## RELATED WORK

- **Grid-free Monte Carlo PDE solvers.** Sawhney & Crane (2020) revived Walk-on-Spheres (WoS) [Muller 1956] for (screened) Poisson problems on complex geometry with spatial acceleration and gradient estimators; Walk-on-Stars extended it to Neumann (Sawhney et al. 2023) and Robin (Miller et al. 2024) conditions.
- **Walk-on-Boundary (WoB).** Sugimoto et al. (2023) gave a practical WoB estimator from boundary integral equations handling Dirichlet/Neumann/Robin data, reviving Sabelfeld & Simonov's random-walk-on-boundary; convergence is limited in non-convex domains and for indefinite operators.
- **Bidirectional / velocity-based estimators.** Qi et al. (2022) recast WoS as reverse/bidirectional walks (reverse-walk splatting) by analogy to bidirectional path tracing; Sugimoto et al. (2024) developed velocity-based WoB estimators for fluids using efficient forward line-integration.
- **Convergent Born Series (CBS) preconditioning.** Osnabrugge et al. (2016) introduced a complex reference wavenumber $k_0^2 + i\varepsilon$ and preconditioner $\gamma = (i/\varepsilon)V$, yielding iteration operator $M \equiv \gamma G V - \gamma + I$ for which *"this combination of $\gamma$ and $\epsilon$ ensures that $\rho(M) < 1$"* — an unconditionally convergent modified Born series and the direct inspiration for our complex shift $\omega$.
- **Learned / neural PDE solvers for Helmholtz.** Stanziola et al. (2023) unrolled a Learned Born Series; Raissi et al. (2019) introduced PINNs; Li et al. (2021) introduced the Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) for parametric PDEs.
- **Neural caching in rendering as an analog for $\hat\mu$.** Li et al. (2023) introduced neural caches for Monte Carlo PDE solvers; Hadadan et al. (2021) solve the rendering equation by minimizing its residual with a neural field (Neural Radiosity); Miller et al. (2023) cache boundary values for WoS.
- **Neural wavefield learning.** Feng et al. (2023, NeuWS) use neural signal representations to reconstruct wavefields through static and dynamic scattering media.
- **Probabilistic Helmholtz precedent.** Yang, Rasila & Sottinen (2017) derived a WoS algorithm for the Helmholtz and Yukawa equations, showing that *"a constant-potential time-independent Schrödinger equation with Dirichlet boundary data can be reformulated as a Laplace equation... which we call the Duffin correspondence"* — a Feynman–Kac-type stochastic representation of the time-harmonic problem.

