**Interpretation:**
- Neumann-series WoB diverges for $\rho(A)\ge 1$ exactly as predicted by the series convergence condition — the empirical crossover at $\rho=1$ validates the failure diagnosis and mirrors the divergence of the classical Born series for high-contrast media.
- Source iteration with a tuned complex $\omega$ converges throughout the sweep, including $\rho(A)>1$, because $\omega$ is selected so that $\rho(M)=\rho\big(I-\omega(I-A)\big)<1$ — the discrete analog of the CBS shift guaranteeing $\rho(M)<1$.
- The observed convergence rate is expected to track the CBS pseudo-propagation law $\Delta r = 2k_0/\varepsilon$: a larger shift magnitude buys guaranteed convergence at the cost of a slower per-iteration rate, reproducing the CBS robustness/speed trade-off.
- Per-iteration variance remains bounded because each iterate uses a single forward WoB apply of $A$ (no $A^n$ composition), avoiding the exponential variance blow-up of deep Neumann walks.
- Warm-starting $\mu^0$ (geometric heuristic or neural $\hat\mu$) is expected to reduce iteration counts, most visibly near $\rho\approx 1$ where unpreconditioned rates are worst.

